FILE: examples/modal/index.html, examples/modal/main.js, examples/modal/README.md
FEATURE: modal-example-expansion
DATE: 2026-08-04
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'modal-stacking'

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — index.html: from three modals to seven
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
(three modals: loginModal, nestedModal, tallModal; loginModal carried the only
nested triggers, and the deepest reachable stack was two layers)

NEW_SECTION
(seven modals)
  loginModal    - page trigger; nested triggers for reset and self-open
  nestedModal   - opened from loginModal; nested trigger for terms
  termsModal    - opened from nestedModal, giving a three layer chain
  profileModal  - independent page trigger
  confirmModal  - opened from profileModal (a confirm over a form)
  helpModal     - opened from profileModal by a SECOND ModalDisplayer instance
  tallModal     - page trigger; taller than the viewport

REASON
Requested: the example should show multiple modals. Three modals could only ever
demonstrate a two layer stack from a single entry point, which does not exercise the
behaviour the module now has.

The additions are chosen so each one shows something the others do not, rather than
being more of the same. termsModal makes the chain three deep, which is where
last-in-first-out ordering becomes visible rather than merely stated. profileModal is
a second independent entry point, so the stack is not always rooted in the same
modal. confirmModal is the realistic "confirm over a form" shape. helpModal is opened
by a separate instance and is the only way to see, in the browser, that the stack is
shared across instances and that one Escape still pops exactly one layer - documented
behaviour that previously had no demonstration.

Existing ids, classes and structure were deliberately preserved (loginModal keeps
exactly two inputs, nestedModal keeps its "Nested" heading text and an input, the
open-nested / open-self / modal-close / data-widths hooks are unchanged) so that the
automated suites, which drive this page as their fixture, continue to address the
same elements.

Each modal carries a distinct accent colour, so that when layers stack it is
immediately obvious which is which rather than requiring the reader to count.

================================================================================
CHANGE 2 — index.html: overlay background selector
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
(no control; the demo only ever ran in the default "color" mode, and
config.overlayBackgroundType = "blur" was undemonstrated)

NEW_SECTION
            <fieldset>
                <legend>Overlay background</legend>
                <label>
                    <select id="overlayType">
                        <option value="color">color — tint the overlay</option>
                        <option value="blur">blur — blur the page behind instead</option>
                    </select>
                </label>
                <p class="hint" ...>
                    Blur applies the <code>vxKit</code> class to <code>config.pageContainer</code>
                    (here <code>#page</code>). The container must wrap the page but not the modal
                    content, or the modal blurs with it. Takes effect on the next open.
                </p>
            </fieldset>

REASON
Requested: add the blur option. Blur has existed in the module since v4 and is listed
in ChangeLog.md, but nothing in the repository showed it working, and it carries two
constraints that are easy to get wrong and invisible from the API alone: the
container must exclude the modal markup, and the setting is snapshotted per layer so
it applies from the next open rather than to the modal already on screen. Both are
stated inline next to the control, where someone experimenting will actually read
them.

The demo page suits this without restructuring, because its modal markup already
sits outside #page - so #page is a valid pageContainer as-is.

================================================================================
CHANGE 3 — main.js: second instance, depth readout, blur wiring
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
const modal = new ModalDisplayer();
...
modal.config.openProcessor = () => log("opened: " + (modal.mainForm ? modal.mainForm.id : "?"));
modal.config.closeProcessor = () => log("closed");
modal.initialize();

document.getElementById("effect").addEventListener("change", e => {
    modal.config.effect = e.target.value;
});

window.demoModal = modal;

NEW_SECTION
(main instance, plus)

const altModal = new ModalDisplayer();
altModal.config.className = "alt-modal";
altModal.config.formIdAttribute = "data-form";
altModal.config.closeButtonClass = "modal-close";
altModal.config.effect = "flip";
altModal.config.overlayStyle = "hsla(180, 40%, 10%, 0.55)";
...
altModal.initialize();

document.getElementById("overlayType").addEventListener("change", e => {
    [modal, altModal].forEach(instance => {
        instance.config.overlayBackgroundType = e.target.value;
        if (e.target.value === "blur") instance.config.pageContainer = document.getElementById("page");
    });
    log("overlay background: " + e.target.value);
});

window.demoModal = modal;
window.altModal = altModal;

REASON
The second instance is given a different trigger class, effect and overlay tint
precisely so that its layer is visibly distinct: it demonstrates that two instances
with unrelated configuration still cooperate on one stack. Both are exposed on
window so the shared depth can be compared from the console.

The callbacks now report depth alongside the modal id, and update an on-page counter,
which turns stacking from something the reader has to infer into something displayed.

================================================================================
CHANGE 4 — closeAll() moved from the page into the modals
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
                <p>Open layers: <span id="depth">0</span>
                   <button type="button" id="closeAllBtn">closeAll()</button></p>
...
document.getElementById("closeAllBtn").addEventListener("click", () => {
    modal.closeAll();
    showDepth();
});

NEW_SECTION
                <p>Open layers: <span id="depth">0</span></p>
                <p class="hint" ...>
                    A page button cannot dismiss a stack — the overlay blocks the page, which is
                    the whole point of a modal. So the <code>closeAll()</code> button lives
                    <em>inside</em> the deeper modals, where it is actually reachable.
                </p>
...
(a `.close-all` button inside termsModal and confirmModal, wired by delegation)
document.addEventListener("click", e => {
    if (e.target.closest(".close-all") != null) {
        modal.closeAll();
        showDepth();
    }
});

REASON
The page level button was unusable and the automated run proved it: Playwright timed
out trying to click it, reporting that the overlay intercepted pointer events. That
is correct modal behaviour, not a defect - a modal blocks the page beneath it - but
it made the control pointless exactly when it was needed, since closeAll() only has
a purpose while a stack is open.

The button therefore moved inside the two deepest modals, where the top layer makes
it reachable. The page text now explains why, which turns a dead control into a
demonstration of modality.

The listener is delegated from document rather than bound to the button, because the
component copies modal content into the overlay: a listener attached to the original
node would not be present on the copy the user actually clicks. This is the same
constraint the guide documents for consumer event handling, so the example now
follows its own advice.

================================================================================
CHANGE 5 — README.md rewritten around the expanded demo
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
(a flat bullet list describing three modals)

NEW_SECTION
(a table mapping each of the seven modals to where it is opened from and what it
shows, followed by a feature list covering both overlay modes, stacking depth, the
shared stack across instances, and why closeAll() sits inside a modal)

REASON
With seven modals and two instances, a flat list no longer tells a reader where to
click. The table answers "what should I open, and why" directly. The feature list
gained the blur mode, the cross-instance stack and the closeAll placement rationale,
and now mentions that the page keeps its layout as well as its scroll position, which
is true only since the scroll lock changed to overflow (see changelog/modal-scroll-lock).
